@node Interface tour
@chapter Interface tour

@cindex interface
@cindex menus
@cindex mouse
@cindex F10

The xwpe interface follows the Borland Turbo C model: a menu bar at the
top, an editor area in the centre, and a status line at the bottom.

@section Activating the menu

Press @key{F10}, @kbd{Alt-@key{SPC}}, or @key{ESC} to activate the menu
bar.  Once active, use the arrow keys to navigate, @key{Enter} to select,
and @key{ESC} to cancel.

Each menu item has a highlighted letter.  Press @kbd{Alt-@var{letter}}
from anywhere to open that menu directly.

@cindex dialog text fields, full
A text field in a dialog holds a fixed number of bytes.  A character typed or
pasted past that is refused, and the status row reads @samp{Input at its limit
(@var{N} bytes)} with the field's size, where the original editor dropped the
character without a word.

@section The menu system

@cindex System menu
@cindex File menu
@cindex Edit menu

xwpe has 10 top-level menus (11 in programming mode).  The @samp{Run},
@samp{Debug}, and @samp{Project} menus only appear in @command{wpe} and
@command{xwpe}, not in @command{we} or @command{xwe}.

@subsection System menu (@kbd{Alt-#})

@table @kbd
@item A -- About
Display version and copyright information.
@item C -- Clear Desktop
Close all open windows.
@item R -- Repaint Desktop
Reinitialise the screen (useful after display corruption).
@item S -- System Info
Display system information.
@item W -- Show Wastebasket
Display deleted files in the wastebasket.
@item D -- Delete Wastebasket
Permanently remove all files in the wastebasket.
@end table

@subsection File menu (@kbd{Alt-F})

@table @kbd
@item M -- File-Manager
Open the file manager (@pxref{Editor,,File manager}).
@item D -- Add File-Manager
Open an additional file manager window.  (Documented since v1.4.)
@item N -- New
Open a new file named ``Noname''.
@item S -- Save
Save the current file.
@item A -- Save As
Save the current file under a new name.
@item L -- Save All
Save all modified files.
@item E -- Execute
Open the execution manager.
@item H -- SHell
Start a UNIX shell (the one named by @env{SHELL}).  In the terminal the
editor hands the shell the whole terminal in its normal state: mouse
tracking, bracketed paste and the alternate screen are switched off and the
line discipline is the cooked one the terminal had before the editor
started, so typing, pasting and the mouse behave as in any other shell.
When the shell exits (@command{exit} or @kbd{Ctrl-D}) the editor repaints
its screen from its own buffer and switches its modes back on.
@item F -- Find
Search for a file by name.
@item G -- Grep
Search for a string or regular expression across files
(@pxref{Editor,,Search and replace}).
@item P -- Print
Print the current file (uses the @env{PRNTCMD} setting).  The print
command gets the file's name as its argument and has a minute to take
the file; a command that takes longer than a second is named on the
status row with that limit.
@item Q -- Quit
Exit xwpe.  Prompts to save unsaved files.
@end table

@subsection Edit menu (@kbd{Alt-E})

@table @kbd
@item T -- Cut
Delete marked text and copy it to the clipboard.
@item C -- Copy
Copy marked text to the clipboard.
@item P -- Paste
Insert clipboard contents at the cursor.
@item S -- Show Buffer
Display the clipboard / paste buffer in a window (@kbd{Alt-Y}).
@item D -- Delete
Delete marked text without copying.
@item U -- Undo
Undo the last change.
@item R -- Redo
Redo the last undone change.
@end table

@subsection Search menu (@kbd{Alt-S})

@table @kbd
@item F -- Find
Search for a string or regular expression (@pxref{Editor,,Search and replace}).
@item R -- Replace
Search and replace.
@item S -- Search again
Repeat the last search.
@item G -- Go to Line
Jump to a specific line number; a number past the last line, however many
digits it has, goes to the last line.  In the programming mode (@command{wpe},
@command{xwpe}) @kbd{Alt-G} is the assistant, so Go to Line is reached here,
from the Search menu; only @command{we} also has it on @kbd{Alt-G}.
@end table

@subsection Block menu (@kbd{Alt-B})

@table @kbd
@item B -- Begin Mark
Set the beginning of a block.
@item E -- End Mark
Set the end of a block.
@item L -- Mark Line
Mark the current line.
@item W -- Mark Whole
Mark the entire document.
@item G -- Goto Begin
Jump to the beginning of the block.
@item N -- Goto End
Jump to the end of the block.
@item C -- Copy
Copy the block to the cursor position.
@item M -- Move
Move the block to the cursor position.
@item D -- Delete
Delete the block.
@item H -- Hide
Remove block marks without deleting text.
@item R -- Read
Insert a file at the cursor position.
@item W -- Write
Write the block to a file.
@item I -- Move to Right
Shift the block right (indent).
@item T -- Move to Left
Shift the block left (unindent).
@end table

@subsection Run menu (@kbd{Alt-R}) -- programming mode only

@cindex compiling
@cindex F9

@table @kbd
@item C -- Compile (@kbd{Alt-C})
Compile the current file.
@item M -- Make (@key{F9})
Build the executable from all project modules.
@item R -- Run (@kbd{Ctrl-F9})
Build and run the executable.
@item I -- Install
Run the install target from the project file.
@item E -- Execute make
Run @command{make(1)} directly.
@item N -- Next Error
Jump to the next error in the message window.
@item P -- Previous Error
Jump to the previous error.
@item S -- Show Definition
Find the definition of the expression at the cursor (C only).
@item X -- Show Next Def.
Find the next definition.
@item K -- Matching Bracket
Find the matching bracket (C only).
@item B -- Beautify
Reformat C source code.
@item A -- Arguments
Enter command-line arguments for the program.
@end table

@subsection Debug menu (@kbd{Alt-D}) -- programming mode only

@cindex debugging
@cindex breakpoints

@table @kbd
@item B -- Toggle Breakpoint (@kbd{Ctrl-F8})
Set or remove a breakpoint at the current line.
@item M -- Remove all Breakpoints
Remove all breakpoints.
@item W -- Make Watch (@kbd{Ctrl-F7})
Add a variable to the watch window.
@item E -- Edit Watch
Modify a watched variable expression.
@item D -- Delete Watch
Remove a watched variable.
@item A -- Remove All Watches
Remove all watched variables.
@item K -- Show Stack (@kbd{Ctrl-F6})
Display the call stack.
@item G -- Goto cursor
Execute until the cursor position is reached.
@item F -- Finish Function
Execute until the current function returns.
@item T -- Trace (@key{F7})
Execute the next line, stepping into functions.
@item S -- Step (@key{F8})
Execute the next line, stepping over functions.
@item R -- Run/Continue (@kbd{Ctrl-F10})
Start or continue the debugger.
@item I -- Interrupt (pause) (@kbd{Ctrl-Break}, @kbd{Ctrl-G I})
Stop the program that is running, where it is.
@item Q -- Quit (@kbd{Ctrl-F2})
Exit the debugger.
@end table

@subsection Project menu (@kbd{Alt-P}) -- programming mode only

@table @kbd
@item P -- Open Project
Load a project file.
@item C -- Close Project
Stop using the current project.
@item A -- Add Item
Add a file to the project.
@item D -- Delete Item
Remove a file from the project.
@item O -- Options
Edit project options (compiler, flags, etc.).
@end table

@subsection Options menu (@kbd{Alt-O})

@table @kbd
@item A -- Adjust Colors
Change the colour scheme.
@item R -- Read Colors
Reset to the default colours or re-read from the initialisation file.
(Documented since v1.4.)
@item S -- Save Options
Save all options to @file{xwperc}.
@item E -- Editor
Editor options (tab size, auto-indent, etc.).
@item F -- File-Manager
File manager options.
@item G -- Programming
Programming environment options.
@item C -- Compiler
Compiler options (path, flags, message format).
@item D -- Debugger
Select the debugger (gdb, sdb, or dbx).
@end table

@subsection Window menu (@kbd{Alt-W})

@table @kbd
@item S -- Size/Move
Resize or move the current window.
@item Z -- Zoom
Maximise the current window.
@item T -- Tile
Tile the file windows.  In the programming mode the @samp{Messages} and
@samp{Watches} windows and the AI pane keep their place at the bottom of the
screen and the tiles end above them (the ``Old Style'' option in
Options > Editor tiles every window).
@item A -- Cascade
Cascade the file windows; the windows docked at the bottom stay where they
are, as under Tile.
@item X -- Next
Cycle to the next window (@kbd{F6} or @kbd{Alt-N}).  To jump straight to a
particular window instead of cycling, press @kbd{Alt-1}@dots{}@kbd{Alt-9}:
the number is the window's index (the source file is window@tie{}1), so the
jump is the same regardless of how many other windows are open.
@item C -- Close
Close the current window (@kbd{Ctrl-W}; the close box in the upper-right
corner does the same).
@item L -- List All
Display a list of all open windows.
@item O -- Output Window
Display the output window.
@item M -- Messages
Display the compiler message window.
@item P -- Project
Display the project window.
@item W -- Watches
Display the watch window.
@end table

@subsection Help menu (@kbd{Alt-H})

@table @kbd
@item E -- Editor
Open the in-app editor help.
@item T -- Topic Search
Search UNIX man pages.  A page that takes longer than a second to make is
named on the status row; @command{man} has 30 seconds at most.
@item U -- Function Index
Display the function index.
@item I -- Info
Browse GNU info nodes.
@item G -- Goto
Jump to a specific help page.
@item B -- Back
Return to the previous help page.
@item N -- Next
Go to the next help page.
@item P -- Previous
Go to the previous help page.
@end table

@section Mouse support

@cindex mouse
@cindex GPM

In terminal mode, xwpe reads the mouse from the terminal's own reports (the
SGR protocol) whenever this terminal is not the console that GPM serves: any
terminal emulator, and any SSH, @command{tmux} or @command{screen} session.  On
a bare Linux virtual console it uses the GPM library instead, which is what
draws the pointer there.  Which of the two applies is decided by asking the
kernel about the terminal, never by @env{TERM}.  In X11 mode, the mouse works
natively.

@itemize @bullet
@item Click on a menu item to activate it.
@item Click in the editor area to position the cursor.
@item Drag to select text (mark a block).
@item @kbd{Shift}-click to extend a selection (X11).
@item Click on window borders to resize or move windows.
@item Click on the scrollbar to scroll.
@end itemize

@subsection Mouse in the editor window

The following describes the mouse buttons in the editor window.
(Documented since v1.4.)

@subsubheading Left mouse button

@itemize @bullet
@item One click moves the cursor to that position.
@item Clicking on the text cursor (current position) marks the word
under the cursor.  Clicking again on an already-marked word selects
the entire line.
@item Hold the button and drag to mark a block.  If @key{Shift} is
also held, the new selection extends the previous one.
@end itemize

@subsubheading Right mouse button

@itemize @bullet
@item Click the right button to copy the marked text into the
clipboard.  Hold @key{Alt} to use the X selection buffer instead.
@item Hold @key{Shift} and click the right button to @emph{cut} the
marked text into the buffer.
@end itemize

@subsubheading Middle mouse button

@itemize @bullet
@item Click the middle button to paste the clipboard contents at the
mouse position.  Hold @key{Alt} to paste from the X selection buffer
instead.
@end itemize

@subsubheading Mouse wheel

@itemize @bullet
@item A wheel step scrolls the window three lines, in the terminal as
under X11 and Wayland; a caret that scrolls out of view follows into the
visible part.  In the AI pane the wheel browses the transcript and keeps
the place as @key{PgUp} and @key{PgDn} do (@pxref{AI assistant}).
@end itemize

@subsubheading Window frame controls

@itemize @bullet
@item Click the @samp{[z]} button (upper right) to zoom the window.
@item Click and drag the upper frame to move the window.
@item Click and drag an edge to resize the window.
@item Click the line number on the lower frame to go to a line number
(a dialog opens to enter the number).
@item Click the scrollbar arrows to scroll one line/column.  Click the
scrollbar body to scroll one page.  Drag the scrollbar marker to scroll
proportionally.
@end itemize

@subsection Mouse in the file manager

(Documented since v1.4.)

@subsubheading Left mouse button

@itemize @bullet
@item One click marks an unselected file or directory.
@item Clicking an already-marked file opens it in the editor.
@item Clicking an already-marked directory makes it the working directory.
@item Hold the button and drag a marked file into another file manager
window to @emph{move} it.
@end itemize

@subsubheading Right mouse button

@itemize @bullet
@item Click an already-marked file or directory to invoke the
Move/Rename function.
@item Drag a file or directory into another file manager window to
create a @emph{link} in the target directory.
@end itemize

@subsubheading Middle mouse button

@itemize @bullet
@item Click on a marked file or directory to @emph{duplicate} it in the
same directory.
@item Hold the button and drag into another file manager window to
@emph{copy} it to that directory.
@end itemize

@section Windows

@cindex windows
@cindex tiling

xwpe can display up to 35 files simultaneously, each in its own window.
Windows can be tiled (@kbd{Alt-W T}), cascaded (@kbd{Alt-W A}), zoomed
(@kbd{Alt-W Z}), or manually resized (@kbd{Alt-W S}).

Each window has:
@itemize @bullet
@item A title bar showing the file name and a close button.
@item Horizontal and vertical scrollbars.
@item A status line showing the cursor position and edit mode.
@end itemize

@section What arrives while a box is open

@cindex dialog, output while open
@cindex box, what waits for it
A box keeps the screen: what arrives meanwhile is shown when it closes.
While a dialog, a dropdown, an error box, the assistant's review box or
a window with keys of its own (the File Manager, the Window list) is in
front, xwpe draws nothing under it.  The program's output goes on into
Messages unseen, a stop, the end of a build, the assistant's reply and a
language server's answer wait, and all of it is shown, in the order it
came, as soon as you are back in a source window.  The same holds while a
command run from the editor has the terminal, while the menu bar
(@kbd{F10}) is open, while a prefix (@kbd{Ctrl-G}, @kbd{Ctrl-K},
@kbd{Ctrl-O}, @kbd{Alt-Q}) waits for its letter, while you move or size a
window, drag a scrollbar, or look at the User Screen (@kbd{Alt-F5}).  A
stop that comes while @kbd{Ctrl-G} waits for its letter is shown after
the command: the letter is taken for the program as you saw it, still
running.

@cindex run, what waits for it
The program you start with @kbd{Ctrl-F9} keeps the screen too, while it
runs in Messages: an assistant reply that lands meanwhile does not open its
review over the program, which would take your typing for the box's keys.
It is shown when the program ends.  The build's output, the assistant's
waiting line, what a language server found (the @samp{LSP: 1 error(s)}
line and the marks on the source) and a message that the server stopped
are kept the same way while a dialog or a dropdown is open, and drawn
when it closes, not at your next key.  A status line you were meant to
read stays until you press a key, however many keys the menu bar or the
size and move command reads first.

@cindex status line, while a box is open
@cindex waiting line, while a box is open
Lines the editor says by itself on the status line wait for a box too:
how long it has waited for a debugger, a build server or a language
server, why it gave up waiting, and that a program stopped reading its
input.  A waiting line comes back with the seconds counted on to the
moment the box closed, and a line meant to be read stays until your next
key.

@cindex resize, while a box is open
If the terminal or the window changes size while a box is open, the
windows are laid out for the new size and, for the dropdowns, the About
and System Info boxes, error boxes, dialogs and the box of a long file
operation (Remove, Rename, Copy), the box is drawn again over them;
when it closes you see the windows as they are now, not what was behind
the box before.  Each box is placed again by its own rule for the new
size: a dialog and an error box are centred again, and the About and
System Info boxes are centred between the menu bar and the status line,
whatever the size.  A dialog you moved with @kbd{Alt-F2} or the mouse
keeps the place you gave it, also after a picker has floated over it,
until the next resize centres it again.  On a screen too small for a box
the About and System Info boxes are cut at the top left, and a dialog
shows only the fields and buttons that fit inside its frame until you
press @kbd{Esc} or make the screen bigger.  A question with a very long
text (Save Options in a deep folder) is cut to the rows the screen has
when it opens and ends in @samp{...}, so its buttons are on the screen.  A dropdown that no longer fits under the
menu bar closes.  The pickers are not laid out again yet.  A resize
closes the language server popups (hover, signature help and the
problem shown at a jump), the reason box of the model picker and the
User Screen, and ends the wait for Enter after a command on the
terminal.  When that wait ends, the editor comes back whole at the new
size, with the status line on the last row: the picture of the screen
saved before the command describes the old size and is not put back.
